data: {"delta":{"partial_json":"{\"file_path\": \"/home/<USER:1>/.local/share/cs-campaign/output/report.md\", \"content\": \"# Campaign report: greet, tested\\n\\n## Mission\\nDeliver `src/greet.py` with `greet(name)` returning `\\\"Hello, \u003cname\u003e!\\\"` and\\n`tests/test_greet.py` covering a normal name and the empty string, with\\n`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` exiting 0.\\n\\n## Outcome\\nAll three criteria met on the first dispatch to `dev`.\\n\\n- `src/greet.py`: `def greet(name): return f\\\"Hello, {name}!\\\"`\\n- `tests/test_greet.py`: `unittest.TestCase` with `test_normal_name`\\n  (`greet(\\\"Alice\\\") == \\\"Hello, Alice!\\\"`) and `test_empty_string`\\n  (`greet(\\\"\\\") == \\\"Hello, !\\\"`), importing `greet` via a `sys.path` adjustment\\n  (repo had no existing package/src layout convention to follow).\\n- Verified independently: fetched dev's branch\\n  (`refs/remotes/campaign/dev/subject`, commit `0a4b10d`), checked out in an\\n  isolated worktree (not dev's own working copy), and ran\\n  `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -v` myself — both tests passed,\\n  exit code 0.\\n\\nDev's own reply also reported having run the same command with the same\\nresult before committing; independent verification confirmed it.\\n\\n## Process notes\\n- One dispatch to dev (`d002`) was sufficient — no rework needed.\\n- Dev's reply flagged `\\\"dirty\\\": true` on its working tree at reply time;\\n  this did not affect the accepted commit, which was clean and matched\\n  exactly the two files described (`git show --stat` confirmed only\\n  `src/greet.py` and `tests/test_greet.py` were added).\\n- No other repository files were touched, per dev's brief.\\n\\n## Verdict\\nCampaign mission met in full: `campaign-met`.\\n\"}","type":"input_json_delta"},"index":0,"type":"content_block_delta"}

